A_654 - There are many reasons for the use of horses and their almost total disappearance in favor of paved roads and automobiles. It's likely that cutting their tails to make violin bows and using nails in horseshoes have made them unstable and less easily tamed. It's easy to imagine that, to be comfortable for horses, roads should be managed so that there are no rocks of any kind (because if a horse is galloping, a rock can be a big problem, and even if it were shod, it wouldn't be at all pleasant for it). Furthermore, shoeing horses is a dangerous practice in my opinion due to the use of nails, and it would be better to use another system to make "shoes" for horses, if you want to use them. Furthermore, there is It's likely that any excrement scattered around should have been collected and used as fertilizer for vegetable gardens (as should be done with dog waste, which is sometimes abandoned), and that this profession was perhaps also mocked and denigrated. As for docking horses' tails, even though we know there are no nerves in it, it's true that the horse feels the weight of it, and if it's cut, it clearly senses that something is wrong, and consequently becomes skittish. A horse's tail serves both an aesthetic purpose, useful during lovemaking, and that it serves as a tool for chasing away flies near its rump (the bites of certain flies can be very painful). As for nails in the hoof, however, it's easy to imagine that if, due to impacts with the ground, the crack in the nail reaches the flesh, the horse feels pain, and in that case too, problems arise. As for the bows of musical instruments, instead of horsehair you can probably use fishing silk; that should also work as it is very strong.